@page "/about"
@inject TransitService Transit

<PageTitle>How Dwellio works — Dwellio</PageTitle>

<div class="shell page narrow stack">
    <span class="eyebrow">Transparency</span>
    <h1>How Dwellio works</h1>
    <p class="lead">Dwellio helps students and newcomers choose where to live in Astana around the place they go every day. Everything below explains where the numbers come from and where they stop being reliable.</p>

    <section class="panel stack">
        <h2>1. Places you want nearby</h2>
        <p>Gyms, saunas, cafés, groceries and nine more categories come from <strong>OpenStreetMap</strong>. A city-wide snapshot ships with the app so it opens instantly and works offline. When you plan around a place, Dwellio asks the free <strong>Overpass API</strong> for fresher data within @(2.5) km and keeps the answer in a local cache for three days. Public Overpass servers are often busy, so several mirrors are tried; if all fail, the snapshot is used and the map label says “Snapshot”.</p>
        <p>Walking time = straight-line distance × @GeoMath.DetourFactor (streets are not straight) ÷ @((int)GeoMath.WalkMetersPerMinute) m per minute.</p>
        <p class="note">OpenStreetMap is edited by volunteers. Some places are missing (Astana has few gyms mapped, for example), and a place on the map may have closed. “Not found” means “not mapped”, not “does not exist”.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="panel stack" id="seats">
        <h2>2. Bus times and your chance of a seat</h2>
        <p>Bus timing comes from an open dataset of real GPS traces of Astana buses <strong>@string.Join(", ", Transit.RouteRefs)</strong> (July–September 2024): <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.15769359" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mansurova et al., Zenodo, CC BY 4.0</a>. Dwellio uses the median time between stops for each hour of the day.</p>
        <p>No open source in Kazakhstan publishes how many people are on a bus. Dwellio therefore <strong>estimates</strong> it from how long buses stand at each stop:</p>
        <ol>
            <li>Passengers at a stop ≈ (dwell time − @BusAssumptions.Default.DoorSeconds s for the doors) ÷ @BusAssumptions.Default.SecondsPerPassenger s per person.</li>
            <li>Near the start of a route most of them board; near the end most get off (a linear split).</li>
            <li>Walking along the route stop by stop (a fold), this gives the average number on board.</li>
            <li>Buses vary a lot, so the seat chance is a probability around that average, compared with @BusAssumptions.Default.Seats seats. It never shows more than 95% or less than 2%.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>The planner then compares tactics: the nearest stop, walking back to an earlier stop (fewer people on board), and leaving about an hour earlier. It assumes a bus every @BusAssumptions.Default.HeadwayMinutes minutes for waiting time.</p>
        <p class="note">This is a model, not a live count, and the data is from 2024. Routes may have changed. Trips not covered by these three buses get a clearly labelled estimate between real OpenStreetMap stops, without a seat chance.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="panel stack">
        <h2>3. Homes and the fit score</h2>
        <p><strong>✔ Real listings</strong> are asking rents published on krisha.kz (reviewed @SeedData.SourcesReviewedOn.ToString("dd.MM.yyyy")), linked to the original page. <strong>Sample price</strong> homes are real complexes at their real location with an illustrative rent, so you can see how the area and commute score. Owners can publish their own listings.</p>
        <p>Must-haves, budget, rooms and the commute limit exclude a home — and Dwellio shows you why. Eligible homes get a 0–100 fit score: price headroom, how many of your needs are within walking distance, and commute time, each multiplied by the weight you choose. Unknown information never counts as a positive.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="panel stack">
        <h2>4. Privacy</h2>
        <p>Your plan is stored in your browser. If you sign in and press “Save plan to my profile”, it is stored in your account. Address searches are sent to OpenStreetMap's Nominatim (or Photon) without any personal data. Passwords are hashed by ASP.NET Core Identity.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="panel stack">
        <h2>5. Technology</h2>
        <p>C# / .NET 10, Blazor (interactive server), Entity Framework Core with SQLite, Leaflet maps. All decisions — classifying places, the spatial index, the seat model, the journey planner and the fit score — are pure functions over immutable records in <code>Dwellio.Core</code>, covered by unit tests.</p>
    </section>

    <a class="button" href="/plan">Start planning</a>
</div>
